  "partials": 53,
  "finals": [
    {
      "id": "1-0",
      "startMs": 3235,
      "endMs": 12185,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "This matrix A, I put its eigenvectors in the columns of a matrix S. So S will be the eigenvector matrix."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-1",
      "startMs": 12791,
      "endMs": 13957,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "And I want to look at this."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-2",
      "startMs": 15362,
      "endMs": 18651,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "Magic combination S inverse AS."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-3",
      "startMs": 19530,
      "endMs": 27249,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "So can I show you how that— what happens there? And notice, notice, there's an S inverse."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-4",
      "startMs": 27978,
      "endMs": 37929,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "We have to be able to invert this eigenvector matrix S. So for that, we need n independent eigenvectors."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-5",
      "startMs": 38111,
      "endMs": 39453,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "So that's the—"
    },
    {
      "id": "1-6",
      "startMs": 40541,
      "endMs": 42512,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "That's the case. Okay, so."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-7",
      "startMs": 44085,
      "endMs": 44771,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "Suppose—"
    },
    {
      "id": "1-8",
      "startMs": 47231,
      "endMs": 50859,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "We have n linearly independent eigenvectors."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-9",
      "startMs": 52016,
      "endMs": 52821,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "Eigenvectors."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-10",
      "startMs": 56904,
      "endMs": 57085,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "Of A."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-11",
      "startMs": 60955,
      "endMs": 61501,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "Put them."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-12",
      "startMs": 65021,
      "endMs": 66585,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "In the columns."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-13",
      "startMs": 68756,
      "endMs": 77609,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "Of this matrix S. So I'm naturally going to call that the eigenvector matrix, because it's got the eigenvectors in its columns."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-14",
      "startMs": 78771,
      "endMs": 83970,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "And all I want to do is show you what happens when you multiply A times S."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-15",
      "startMs": 84945,
      "endMs": 85958,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "So A times S."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-16",
      "startMs": 89235,
      "endMs": 97910,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "So this is A times the matrix with the first eigenvector in its first column, the second eigenvector in its second column."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-17",
      "startMs": 98777,
      "endMs": 101265,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "The nth eigenvector in its nth column."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-18",
      "startMs": 102624,
      "endMs": 109055,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "And how am I going to do this matrix multiplication? Well, certainly I'll do it a column at a time."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-19",
      "startMs": 109999,
      "endMs": 110797,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "And what do I get?"
    },
    {
      "id": "1-20",
      "startMs": 112600,
      "endMs": 117019,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "A times the first column gives me the first column of the answer. But what is it?"
    },
    {
      "id": "1-21",
      "startMs": 117835,
      "endMs": 118954,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "That's an eigenvector."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-22",
      "startMs": 120028,
      "endMs": 127312,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "A times x1 is equal to the lambda times the x1. And that lambda we'll call lambda 1, of course."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-23",
      "startMs": 129619,
      "endMs": 138472,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "So that's the first column. Ax1 is the same as lambda 1 x1. Ax2 is lambda 2 x2, so on along to infinity."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-24",
      "startMs": 138634,
      "endMs": 141973,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "In the nth column, we now have lambda n xn."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-25",
      "startMs": 144725,
      "endMs": 147383,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "Looking good, but the next step is even better."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-26",
      "startMs": 148524,
      "endMs": 155265,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "So for the next step, I want to separate out those eigenvalues, those multiplying numbers."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-27",
      "startMs": 156209,
      "endMs": 156947,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "From the x's."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-28",
      "startMs": 158867,
      "endMs": 167865,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "So then I'll have just what I want. OK, so how am I going to separate out? So that number lambda 1 is multiplying the"
    },
    {
      "id": "1-29",
      "startMs": 167882,
      "endMs": 168525,
      "speakerLabel": "PENDING",
      "text": "first column."
    },
    {
      "id": "1-30",
      "startMs": 169756,
      "endMs": 176788,
      "speakerLabel": "A",
      "text": "So if I want to factor it out of the first column, I better put— here is going to be x1."
    }
  ],
